  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Given a curve $x=3y-{y}^{2},$ what is the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point $(0,3)?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(3y-{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =3(1)-2y \\\\  & =3-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3-2y}.\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $(0,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(0,3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3-2(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-3)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point-slope formula for a tangent line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
